# The .rest extension is the same format as .http. This file covers what requests.http leaves out:
# in-file variables, named requests and chaining, dynamic variables, auth shorthand, an external
# body, multipart, and per-request directives. Every request goes to httpbin.org, a public echo
# service, so they need a network connection. Credentials here are fake.

@host = https://httpbin.org
@item = hammer

# A named request: later requests can read its response. It is the first request in the file, so no
# ### separator comes before it.
# @name create
POST {{host}}/post
Content-Type: application/json
X-Request-Id: {{$uuid}}

{
  "item": "{{item}}",
  "createdAt": "{{$isoTimestamp}}",
  "quantity": {{$randomInt}}
}

### Chained: reuses a value from the response to "create"
GET {{host}}/get?item={{create.response.body.$.json.item}}&status={{create.response.status}}
Accept: application/json

### Basic auth shorthand: "user password" is encoded for you
GET {{host}}/basic-auth/ada/not-a-real-password
Authorization: Basic ada not-a-real-password

### External body: the request body is read from the file beside this one
POST {{host}}/post
Content-Type: application/json

< ./body.json

### Multipart form: one inline part and one file part
POST {{host}}/post
Content-Type: multipart/form-data; boundary=sample-boundary

--sample-boundary
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="note"

Sent from {{item}}
--sample-boundary
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="file"; filename="body.json"
Content-Type: application/json

< ./body.json
--sample-boundary--

### Directives: do not follow the redirect, and give up after five seconds
# @no-redirect
# @timeout 5
GET {{host}}/redirect-to?url=https%3A%2F%2Fexample.com

// A request with no method defaults to GET; this line is a comment in the other style.
###
{{host}}/uuid
